{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "את שבתותי תשמרו", :translit "et shabtotai tishmeru", :gloss "My sabbaths you shall keep"} {:he "סמך לע\"א", :translit "samach la'ah", :gloss "juxtaposed to the 71"} {:he "ששקול שבת כנגד ע\"א", :translit "she-shakul shabbat k'neged a'h", :gloss "that Sabbath is weighed against 71"} {:he "את מקדשי תיראו", :translit "et mikdashi tira'u", :gloss "My sanctuary you shall revere"} {:he "אין בנין בה\"מ דוחה שבת", :translim "ein binyan Beit HaMikdash docheh Shabbat", :gloss "building of the Holy Temple does not override Sabbath"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Counting 71 labors against which Sabbath is weighed"} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of Sabbath command and sanctuary command in adjacent verses"}], :frame {:text "The Sages enumerate the primary labors forbidden on Sabbath as 39 (Shabbat 7:2), but the count of 71 appears in talmudic discussion of Sabbath labors (Shabbat 49b–50a). The principle that Temple construction does not override the Sabbath is derived from Exodus 35:2–3, where the Sabbath command immediately precedes the instructions for the Tabernacle, teaching that the work of the Tabernacle yields to Sabbath (Shabbat 49b, Yoma 85b). Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition of Leviticus 26:1–2 similarly: the verse ordering reverence for the sanctuary alongside Sabbath observance signals that same hierarchy.", :sources ["Shabbat 49b" "Yoma 85b" "Exodus 35:2" "Exodus 35:3"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את שבתותי תשמרו", :translit "et shabtotai tishmeru", :gloss "My sabbaths you shall keep"} {:he "שמירה", :translit "shmirah", :gloss "keeping / guarding"} {:he "שבת", :translit "shabbat", :gloss "Sabbath"} {:he "שמור את יום השבת", :translit "shmor et yom ha-shabbat", :gloss "Keep the Sabbath day"} {:he "תוספת מחול על הקודש", :translit "tosafet chol al ha-kodesh", :gloss "addition of profane onto sacred"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Placement of the word 'keep' both after and before 'Sabbath' in different verses signals guarding at both boundaries"}], :frame {:text "The practice of adding time from the profane day onto the sacred day — beginning Sabbath early and concluding it late — is a rabbinic obligation known as tosefet Shabbat (Rosh Hashanah 9a). The juxtaposition of 'My sabbaths you shall keep' in Leviticus 26:2 with 'Keep the Sabbath day' in Deuteronomy 5:12 demonstrates that the root sh-m-r appears on both sides of the word 'Sabbath,' indicating that guarding is required both before and after the day itself.", :sources ["Rosh Hashanah 9a" "Leviticus 26:2" "Deuteronomy 5:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Counting 71 labors against which Sabbath is weighed", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of Sabbath command and sanctuary command in adjacent verses", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Placement of the word 'keep' both after and before 'Sabbath' in different verses signals guarding at both boundaries", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:26:08.998111810Z", :model "glm-5.2"}